# Pattern Relationships

Movement patterns are connected.

Example

Squat

↓

Jump

↓

Sprint

Improving one pattern may influence another.

Relationships are stored explicitly.

---

# Pattern Balance

CAS evaluates balance across patterns.

Examples

Too much Horizontal Push

↓

Increase Horizontal Pull

Too much Hinge

↓

Reduce posterior-chain loading

Movement balance reduces injury risk.

---

# Pattern Priority

Not every pattern receives equal emphasis.

Priority depends on:

Sport

Training phase

Athlete profile

Recovery

Current objectives

---

# Combat Sport Profiles

Every combat sport emphasizes patterns differently.

Example

Boxing

Horizontal Push

Rotation

Footwork

Carry

Example

Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu

Horizontal Pull

Carry

Ground Transition

Bridge

Example

Krav Maga

Balanced development across all primary patterns.

---

# Exercise Independence

Movement patterns survive exercise replacement.

Example

Back Squat unavailable.

↓

Front Squat selected.

↓

Movement objective unchanged.

The athlete still trains the Squat pattern.

---

# Explainability

Movement patterns improve explanations.

Example

Today's exercise changed,

but today's movement objective remained identical.

---

# Learning

CAS learns which exercises best develop each pattern for each athlete.

Movement patterns remain constant.

Exercise selection becomes increasingly individualized.

---

# Future Expansion

Future versions may include:

Movement quality scoring.

Computer vision.

Real-time movement detection.

Automatic pattern recognition.

---

# Final Principle

Exercises change.

Movement patterns remain.

The Combat Athlete System develops movement first,

and selects exercises second.